{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511794-gifts-the-buyer-s-guide","id":1511794,"title":"Gifts: The Buyer's Guide","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-05-16T06:47:07-07:00","id":1511794,"title":"Gifts: The Buyer's Guide","content":"I'm going to break down Gifts by level and category, bit by bit. If it's Wyrmish, Fera-only, obscure or lost it's going to feature much much later, if at all. This is intended to focus on the Gifts you will actually be getting in play, hence starting at level 1 and working up. I'll be considering utility (both inside and outside combat), room for creativity, fun ways to get the most enjoyment out of a Gift, when you should take it, why, and so on. Accessibility is also going to come up when relevant.<br /> <br /> Given how cheap Gifts are, especially the level 1 and 2 gifts, finding value and fun in a great number of them can lead to a fun and varied playstyle. Enjoy and let me know what you think! I'd love to hear if I missed any whacky or obvious applications.<br /> <br /> Let's begin with Rank 1 Homid Gifts!<br /> <br /> Note that I will be dividing Gifts into three descriptive categories intended to give a really quick distillation of my thoughts. Think of it like traffic lights. <span style=\"color:#2ecc71\">Green</span> are must-haves, or possibly must-reworks depending on your table and how adversarial or cheeky the environment is. Do what works for your table of course. <span style=\"color:#f39c12\">Amber</span> are fun, cool, perhaps niche or minor effects, have good RP potential perhaps, and are ultimately worth taking if you like the concept or have spare EXP. Thankfully most Gifts are, based on what I know before starting this thorough review, Green or Amber! <span style=\"color:#e74c3c\">Red</span> are Gifts which are underwhelming, overcosted, too pricey to use, have minor or ultra-niche effects, and don't have any &quot;hook&quot; for RP that makes them worth taking. I'm hoping very, very few will be Red, but I can think of a few!<div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><span style=\"color:#2ecc71\"><u><b>Apecraft's Blessings:</b></u></span> we start off VERY strong here. Even with a modest Wits + Crafts pool, -1 or -2 difficulty to most practical tasks you can imagine is exceptional. This Gift is good enough that it would still be viable at Rank 3 or 4. It's taken very frequently at char-gen and with good reason; it's one of Homid's superpowers, and STs should be cautious about Ancestor Spirits teaching it to Metis and Lupus, even with the extra cost. Driving, shooting, cooking, sewing, pole vaulting, piloting an airplane without any training... the near universal applications are incredible even if you were to limit the difficulty decrease to 1 or 2. RAW there's no reason you shouldn't shoot for a 5 or 6 success Wits + Crafts check.<br /> <br /> If you find this one is creating problems, adding a Gnosis cost or making it once per scene wouldn't be too absurd. See what works at your table.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>City Running:</b></u></span> this one is pretty neat. Spending a point of Rage can hurt especially if you don't have much to spend, but it's a fun ability which makes you impossible to catch for many. Picture someone scaling a fire escape as swiftly as they could run the 100 meters. The difficulty reduction is also nice, and while this one is somewhat niche it's bound to come up in a traditionally urban WoD game; back alleys, fire exits, rooftops and so on are pretty likely places to be chasing Fomori, Vampires and so on.<br /> <br /> On the proactive front, having an urban base which is almost impossible to access without this Gift could be a pretty neat setup. You can also try to drag your confrontations and battles onto scaffolding for cool cinematic fights in which you are much more able to navigate easily.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Master of Fire:</b></u></span> underrated because people don't imagine their Garou burning to death, and because spending Gnosis on a niche ability seems eh. The thing is that while you don't often run through burning buildings, fight hunters with flamethrowers or arsonist blood mages, nor will you frequently run into a Flambeau duelist or a fire-breathing Bane/Fomor, all these threats do add up to coming up at least once per campaign I'd wager, and perhaps much more often than that.<br /> <br /> Aggravated damage is still hellish for Garou, as you can't soak it in your Breed form (unless you're Metis) and you can't regenerate it either. Turning fires into Bashing damage makes them somewhat trivial sources of damage, and also allows you to proactively use fire as a weapon. Yes you won't often have an incendiary grenade thrown at you, but a packmate chucking napalm or a fire-starting Gift at you while you have an enemy pinned down... it's messy and risky, but &quot;I take (3-soak) bashing per turn, you take 3 aggravated damage per turn&quot; is a pretty good trade off and will make you look pretty badass.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#2ecc71\"><b><u>Persuasion:</u></b></span> They did it again! This one's just Apecraft's Blessings but for social stuff! A wonderfully strong social Gift which you'll see being utilized more frequently than any other Gift in some PBP servers, Persuasion is a neat power which could also do fine as a level 3 or 4, not that it's necessarily a problem at level 1. The lack of a Gnosis cost does stand out.<br /> <br /> My only serious suggestion to change this power which should be used at all tables is to note that &quot;has the potential to change long-held views&quot;, &quot;successes are much more powerful than normal social checks&quot; etc. should only apply to regular mortals. If Garou could un-bigot eachother, or re-bigot eachother, the setting would look entirely different. It's also a power which plays up the necessity of having some form of check to notice when supernatural powers are being used, much like Presence does. Garou should consider utilizing this Gift upon other Garou without good justification to be inflammatory.<br /> <br /> Noting its lack of cost and it lasting all scene, the most optimal way to use this Gift is forever, always, in all situations. It is however not very fun. If you are left to police your own usage of this power by a relaxed ST or a server where homebrew is avoided, I'd advise utilizing it only when you can picture the character going &quot;hmm, this is tricky - perhaps I need a little help&quot;. Your Garou will come off as more relatable and less like a character in a game applying their standard buff before a fight.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Smell of Man:</b></u></span> This one is fun and quite niche, and I quite enjoy it. I don't have much to say besides the obvious; it'll be handy when people have guard dogs, angry cats, wild wolves, other animal kinfolk and so on. If you want to actively utilize this power aggressively, dragging a fight you're having into a situation with aggressive domesticated or wild animals will have the favour tilted slightly in your direction, as you are treated like a friend by guard dogs and attacked less savagely by wild animals, but the benefit is very mild compared to the effort you've taken to arrange it. If you are a Galliard this combines very well with Beast Speech, since not only can you talk to domesticated animals they will also treat you like a great friend.<br /> <br /> Much like City Running and Master of Fire, this is one where you can go out of your way to try and trigger it to your advantage, and you can build a fun concept around it. The crazy cat lady Bone Gnawer Galliard, the security firm owning Glass Walker Ragabash who has a charmed touch with vicious guard dogs, the Child of Gaia vet who helps to rehabilitate traumatized and abused animals from fighting rings. If you want the fun RP flex of animals adoring you this is a neat one, and that alone is worth 3 EXP.</div> <br /> <br /> As you can see Homids start off quite strong! Two Greens and three Ambers, and the RP potential and fun held within these offerings is pretty great. Given how prevalent Homids are, it's good that these Gifts are fun to use and RP with; they're some of the most common Gifts in WTA!<br /> <br /> Next up will be Metis Rank 1 Gifts, which should be a very fun one.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}